insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('8ab93566-2387-576b-ba4f-90379ab47c67', 'wg5-informative-writing-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'write-g5-informative-writing'), null,
        '3-5', $q$Reading with Fingertips$q$, $q$(1) Braille is a system of raised dots that people who are blind or have low vision read with their fingers. (2) It was created in France in the 1820s by a teenager who had lost his sight as a young child. (3) Before braille, books for blind readers used large raised letters that were slow to feel and hard to make.

(4) Braille is built from a small unit called a cell. (5) Each cell has room for six dots in two columns of three. (6) Different patterns of raised dots stand for different letters, numbers, and punctuation marks. (7) For example, a single dot in the top left corner stands for the letter a. (8) Readers glide their fingertips across a line of cells, much like eyes move across a page.

(9) Today, braille appears in many places. (10) You can find it on elevator buttons, room signs, and some menus. (11) Electronic braille displays have tiny pins that pop up and down to show words from a computer. (12) Braille is cool.$q$, 'draft')
